{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "1: Database Connections with Password Authentication",
  "nextTour": "2: Enabling Passkeys for Your Database Connection",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Set up tenant and local configurations for this lab",
      "description": "## Set up tenant and local configurations for this lab\n\n\n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.tenantConfigure) to configure your Auth0 tenant for this lab. This will register a Regular Web Application named **Lab: Web App** (if it doesn't already exist) in the tenant you authorized with the Auth0 Labs extension. It will also update the Allowed Callback URLs for your application.\n>Wait until you see the message that `resources.xml` has been successfully deploy before continuing to the next step.\n\n    ![resources deployed message](https://cdn.auth0.com/website/a0fun/v2/resources.png) \n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.localConfigure) to configure your local environment. This creates `.env` file under the `src/web-app` directory with the appropriate environment variables and values for integrating with Auth0.\n\n    These include:\n    - `ISSUER_BASE_URL`\n    - `CLIENT_ID`\n    - `CLIENT_SECRET`\n\n1. If you do not see the `.env` file created in `src/web-app`, check the following:\n    - You authorized the Auth0 Labs extension with your Auth0 Tenant in the first step of this lab.\n    \n     > See https://auth0-training.github.io/#im-missing-an-env-file-for-my-application--my-application-doesnt-launch for more troubleshooting details.\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 4,
          "character": 131
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 4,
          "character": 226
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "What you'll do",
      "description": "## Here's what you'll do:  \n\n*   Explore the default Username-Password Database \n*   Create a new Auth0 Database\n*   Add new users to the database using bulk migration\n\nA connection is a relationship between Auth0 and a source of users, which may include identity providers (such as Google or LinkedIn), databases, or passwordless authentication methods. Auth0 sits between your application and its sources of users, which adds a level of abstraction so your application is isolated from any changes to and idiosyncrasies of each source's implementation."
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Test the default Auth0 database connection",
      "description": "## Test the default Auth0 database connection\n\nAuth0 provides database connections to authenticate users with an email/username and password. These credentials are securely stored in the Auth0 user store or in your own database. A default database connection is set up for you when your tenant was created.\n\nIn this exercise, you will explore the settings for the default database connection, and see what happens if an application has no connection enabled for it.\n\n1. In your tenant, select **Authentication** > **Database**. \n    >The available databae connections are displayed. You should see **Username-Password-Authentication**, which is available by default. This database stores accounts that are created by users or migrated in. Social connections will NOT appear here.\n      \n1. Select **Username-Password-Authentication** to open its settings.     \n     >In Settings, you can require usernames, disable signups, and import users from a legacy store (trickle migration).  \n      \n1. Select the **Authentication Methods** tab and select the button to configure the Password policy.\n    >Here you can customize your specific password policy, including password strength, history, a dictionary of forbidden passwords, and forbidding the use of personal data in passwords.  \n\n      \n1. Select the **Custom Database** tab. \n    >If you have an enterprise account you can enable a Custom Database connection to link to your own MySql, Mongo, SQL Server, etc. database. Here you would also set up your Database Actions scripts for user lifecycle operations:\n    \n    - Login\n    - Create\n    - Verify\n    - Change password\n    - Get user\n    - Delete  \n          \n    However, as you are signed up for a free Auth0 account, this feature will be disabled unless you upgrade to an Enterprise plan.   \n\n1. Select the **Applications** tab, then toggle switch next to the **Lab: Web App** to the off position.  \n\n1. Your app should still be running from the last time we launched it. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.openEndpointByName?[\"Lab: Web App\"]) to open it in your browser.\n    - If it isn't still running, select [this link](command:workbench.action.debug.start) to start it up again.\n1. Select the log in link in the top right corner.\n    >Note that your login screen now only has sign-in with social connections, as you have disabled the database connection for this application. As this change was made in the tenant, and didn't require changes to application code, there was no need to rebuild the application.  \n1. Back in your tenant, re-enable the Username-Password-Authentication connection for **Lab: Web App** by switching the toggle button to the on position.\n  \n1. Select [this button](command:auth0.lab.openEndpointByName?[\"Lab: Web App\"]) to open your web app up again and click the login link. (You can also just refresh the page if you already have it open.)\n    >Username and password authentication is restored!  "
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Create a new Auth0 database connection",
      "description": "## Create a new Auth0 database connection\n\nWe're going to import some user accounts into Auth0 - but before we do, let's create a new database connection for them. \n\n1. Go to **Authentication** > **Database**, and select **Create DB Connection**.\n      \n1. Enter **Contoso-Users** as the name for this database connection.\n\n1. Toggle on **Email Address** as an identifier.\n\n1. Select the **Create** button.\n    \n1. Select the **Applications** tab and enable this new connection for the  Lab: Web App application.  \n      \n1. Go to **Applications** > **Applications** and select **Lab: Web App**\n\n1. Select the **Connections** \n    Note this application is using two database connections:\n    - Username-Password-Authentication \n    - Contoso-Users\n    \n1. Disable the **Username-Password-Authentication** connection for the application by toggling it off.   \n    >This will ensure that only the users we're adding into the new database connection are able to log in. Next, we'll sign up using this new connection.\n"
    },
    {
      "title": "Launch the sample application (Lab: Web App) and test social login",
      "description": "## Launch the sample application (Lab: Web App) and test social login\n\n1. Select [this link](command:workbench.action.debug.start) to launch the application locally.\n    >Wait a moment for the application to launch. You can see the progress in the debug console.\n\n1. Ensure the port for the web app (37500) is set to public. \n    - Click the **PORTS** tab below.\n    - Right click **37500** under the Ports column.\n    - Select **Port Visibility** > **Public**.\n    >Note: You will need to repeat these steps if you restart your Codespace.\n\n    ![](https://cdn.auth0.com/website/auth0/labs/ilt/public-ports.gif)\n\n1. Select [this link](command:auth0.lab.openEndpointByName?[\"Lab: Web App\"]) to open the sample app in your browser.\n\n\n",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 7,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 11,
          "character": 1
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sign up using the Contoso-Users connection",
      "description": "## Sign up using the Contoso-Users connection\n\n1. Select the login link in the app\n    >The login screen is displayed because you have at least one connection enabled (The new Contoso database). But, there is no indication to the user that anything has changed.  \n1. Select the **Sign up** link and enter an email address that you have access to, along with a unique password for this new account. \n    - Use good password hygiene: Don’t use an existing password!\n    - **DO NOT** use social login. Instead, log in using a username and password.   \n    - If you've completed other labs and used a Gmail alias, you will want to increment the alias (+auth0labs2 for example) in order to sign up as a new unique user -- as you will need to receive the verification email. You may also just use any email address you have not used before and have access to. \n        \n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **User Management** > **Users**.  \n    >Note that you have a user with your email address and it is stored in the **Contoso-Users** database connection you created. \n        \n1. Go to **Applications** > **Applications** > **Lab: Web App** > **Connections** and re-enable the **Username-Password-Authentication** connection.\n\n\nWith the new database in place and enabled, we're ready to add some users."
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Add new users to the database using bulk migration",
      "description": "## Add new users to the database using bulk migration\n\nAuth0 supports importing users from external applications using Custom Database connections, the Auth0 Management API, or Auth0 Dashboard.\nIn this exercise, you will perform a bulk user import using the Auth0 Dashboard. Bulk imports are useful for migrating users from an existing database or service to Auth0. \n\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **User Management** > **Users**.\n\n1. Select the **Import/Export Users** button and then select **Import Users**.\n\n1. This feature requires a specially formatted JSON file. Select this link to download the file to use for this lab: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/auth0/auth0-product-education-labs/main/data/bulk-import-users.json\n\n1. Select the **Choose File** button and select the JSON file you just downloaded.\n\n1. Under the **Connection** section, select **Contoso-Users** from the drop-down menu.\n\n1. Select the **Import Users** button.",
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 87,
          "character": 50
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 87,
          "character": 107
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Check that the users were imported",
      "description": "## Check that the users were successfully imported\n1. From the Auth0 Dashboard, go to **User Management** > **Users**.\n1. You should see the newly imported users with a value of **Contoso-Users** under the Connection column.\n\n1. Clean-up: Enable the User-Password-Authentication Database Connection again.\n    1. Go to **Applications** > **Applications** > **Lab: Web App**.\n    1. Select the **Connections** tab.\n    1. Toggle on **User-Password-Authentication**.\n"
    },
    {
      "file": "",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "What you did",
      "description": "## What you did\n\nIn this lab you:\n\n*   Created a new Auth0 database connection.\n*   Enabled the connection for your application.\n*   Imported new users to the new database using bulk migration/user import."
    }
  ]
